WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:15.840
The South Pacific, the first thing that comes to&nbsp;
mind are sublime beaches, luxury resorts and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:19.740
rich cultures of people with cool tattoos.
 
It is as if in this part of the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:24.000
except for the occasional shipwreck, nothing&nbsp;
ever happens, as if time itself has stopped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:27.900
and everything on these remote islands&nbsp;
remains calm, harmonious and peaceful.
 &nbsp;

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:34.440
But, wait a minute, this is VisualPolitik: what&nbsp;
if I told you that the Pacific is once again&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:38.640
becoming an absolutely crucial region for&nbsp;
the geopolitics of the great world powers?
 &nbsp;

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:40.680
You don’t believe me? Well, take a look:
 &nbsp;

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:45.180
N1(China's Foreign Minister is about to meet&nbsp;
with 10 Pacific nations. The outcome could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:49.380
have big ramifications for Australia – ABC)
 
And yes, okay, I know what many of you are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:53.400
thinking at this very moment: who on earth&nbsp;
would be interested in all those tiny,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:57.300
remote islands that dot the South Pacific?
 
It is true, at first glance they do not seem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:01.320
to have anything interesting to offer: they tend&nbsp;
to be rather poor enclaves, highly dependent on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:05.820
tourism and fishing and, to top it off, they&nbsp;
can be extremely vulnerable to global warming.
 &nbsp;

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:09.480
Well, guess what? If you think&nbsp;
this way, you might be very wrong.
 &nbsp;

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:13.440
Think about it, except for the larger and wealthy&nbsp;
islands of Australia and New Zealand, the rest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:17.760
are tiny island States. And then there is Papua&nbsp;
New Guinea, which is quite big but very poor.
 &nbsp;

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:22.680
However, do you know how much total space they&nbsp;
occupy on the world map? Well... Pay attention.
 &nbsp;

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:32.340
We are talking about no less than 15% of&nbsp;
the entire global surface. In other words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:36.780
all these islands have jurisdiction over an&nbsp;
area that is three times larger than the entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:41.880
continental surface of the United States! In this&nbsp;
gigantic space, it is these small countries that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:46.620
have control over any activity that takes place.
 
Surprised? You shouldn't be. After all, if this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:50.820
region wasn't important, why did the former Empire&nbsp;
of Japan put so much effort into taking it over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:54.300
completely during the Second World War?
 
The reality is that the little known and lost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:58.620
South Pacific has an awesome strategic importance.&nbsp;
However, that did not prevent this region of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:03.060
world from falling into oblivion when it began to&nbsp;
become independent between the 1960s and 1980s.
 &nbsp;

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:06.300
But take note, because today&nbsp;
the story has changed again.
 &nbsp;

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:10.980
Since the beginning of the 21st century, China has&nbsp;
been trying to woo small island States to become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:15.360
not only its main partner, but also its main&nbsp;
economic and political model. In fact, that move&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:21.420
is exactly what explains news stories like this:
 
N2(Chinese hospital ship cruises the South Pacific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:23.940
to spread influence – Asia Nikkei)
 
Of course, the United States has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:27.720
started to pay attention, and Australia is&nbsp;
already in fifth gear, which, after all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:33.180
is why they are the great regional power.
 
However, Xi Jinping's regime is offering deals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:38.040
that are almost impossible to refuse and that far&nbsp;
exceed the West's gamble over recent decades.
 &nbsp;

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:42.360
So, the question we can all ask ourselves&nbsp;
is: how far does China's influence in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:46.020
South Pacific extend? Why does this region&nbsp;
suddenly seem so important? Can Australia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:49.980
and the United States regain their political&nbsp;
dominance in this remote part of the world?
 &nbsp;

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:55.500
In this video we will answer all these questions,&nbsp;
but first let's take a look at some history.
 &nbsp;

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:02.100
T1(THE DREAM OF THE SOUTH)
 &nbsp;

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:10.680
I'm sure that, unless you live there, apart&nbsp;
from the occasional travel documentary on TV&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:14.580
or here on YouTube, most of you have never&nbsp;
really stopped to think about this part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:18.840
the world. Many of you may not have even heard of&nbsp;
some of the countries that lie in these waters.
 &nbsp;

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:23.040
And you would certainly never recognize them&nbsp;
as having the slightest political relevance.
 &nbsp;

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:26.580
However, VisualPolitik friends, for a long&nbsp;
time, these islands were much more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:31.620
beach locations for travel programs and dodgy&nbsp;
reality shows: they were strategic enclaves.
 &nbsp;

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:38.160
The South Pacific is divided&nbsp;
into three different regions:
 &nbsp;

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:41.880
On the one hand, there is Polynesia,&nbsp;
which is the largest region and extends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:45.060
to Easter Island itself (you know, the&nbsp;
one with the big stone head monuments);&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:49.860
then there is Micronesia, which, as its&nbsp;
name suggests, has the smallest islands;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:53.820
and finally Melanesia, which is characterized&nbsp;
because its population tends to be darker.
 &nbsp;

00:03:53.820 --> 00:03:57.300
Not surprisingly, none of these three&nbsp;
regions escaped European colonialism.
 &nbsp;

00:03:57.300 --> 00:04:01.440
The first arrival took place in 1513. Its&nbsp;
protagonist was the Spanish explorer and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:06.240
conqueror Vasco Núñez de Balboa. From that moment&nbsp;
on, Dutch, German, French and English voyages&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:09.720
continued to follow one after the other.&nbsp;
In the 18th century, Europeans began to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:16.380
develop plantations, mainly sugar plantations.
 
However, the strategic importance of this region&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:20.400
did not become clear until the two world wars&nbsp;
of the 20th century. It was then that it became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:24.060
evident that the South Pacific is a crucial&nbsp;
region for maintaining Australia's connection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:27.900
to the United States and the rest of Asia.
 
Don't forget that Australia is not only a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:33.120
important ally of the United States, it is also a&nbsp;
huge repository of all kinds of natural resources.

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:37.200
However, once World War Two ended, the South&nbsp;
Pacific ceased to be of interest to the great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:40.860
powers. The islands gained their independence&nbsp;
and Australia became the area's police.
 &nbsp;

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:45.540
During that time, practically the only relevant&nbsp;
movement was the arrival of a few tourists.
 &nbsp;

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:47.220
However, when the day came that the People's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:50.940
Republic of China set its sights on&nbsp;
this region... Everything changed.

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:54.300
Think about it, in the event of a conflict,&nbsp;
Chinese presence in the area could prevent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:58.260
the United States from using Australia as&nbsp;
a resupply center for its operations in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:01.680
region. In other words, by getting a foothold&nbsp;
in this region, China could prevent the growing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:05.340
alliance that Washington is forging with&nbsp;
Canberra and Tokyo from being effective.

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:13.020
Let’s take a look.
 
T2(THE CHINESE DREAM)
 &nbsp;

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:21.540
It has already become a familiar story: in&nbsp;
all those former colonies of Europe that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:25.500
still eminently poor, China is moving like&nbsp;
a fish in water to try to gain advantage.

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:28.620
On VisualPolitik we have already told you&nbsp;
about the growing presence of the Asian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:32.040
giant in Sub-Saharan Africa. Well,&nbsp;
the South Pacific is no different.
 &nbsp;

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:34.980
We are talking about growing ties&nbsp;
that, until relatively recently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:40.380
hardly anyone cared about. However, in 2022 there&nbsp;
was one piece of news that set off all the alarm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.380 --> 00:05:44.580
bells. I'm sure you all remember it:
 
N3(Solomon Islands: China deal in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.580 --> 00:05:48.660
Pacific stokes Australian fears – BBC)
 
Exactly, China finalized its first security&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:51.720
agreement in the region with the Solomon&nbsp;
Islands, which does not mean that it will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:54.720
set up a military base in the country,&nbsp;
but it will have certain privileges.
 &nbsp;

00:05:57.480 --> 00:06:01.980
For example, China will be able to send military&nbsp;
personnel and armed police to secure its projects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.980 --> 00:06:06.480
in the country, and it will also have access to&nbsp;
local ports so that its fleet can resupply there.
 &nbsp;

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:09.660
As you can imagine, Australia's biggest&nbsp;
fear is that China will eventually get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:13.500
around to putting a military base there&nbsp;
that would be less than 2,000 km, that is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.500 --> 00:06:18.060
1,243 miles, from the Australian coast.
 
For the time being, the Prime Minister of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:21.720
Solomon Islands has denied this possibility:
 
C1("Let me assure you all again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:26.100
there is no military base, nor any other&nbsp;
military facility, or institutions in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:29.820
agreement. And I think that's a very important&nbsp;
point that we continue to reiterate to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.820 --> 00:06:34.680
family in the region". – Manasseh Sogavare,&nbsp;
Solomon Islands Prime Minister, July 2022)
 &nbsp;

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:38.580
However, Sogavare's assurances that there will be&nbsp;
no Chinese military bases reassure almost no one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:42.300
The agreement has been a turning point, a profound&nbsp;
change in the South Pacific because, for the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:46.260
time since World War Two, Australia is seeing&nbsp;
a direct challenge to its regional dominance.
 &nbsp;

00:06:46.260 --> 00:06:49.800
And, let’s keep in mind that China&nbsp;
has not stopped with the Solomons.
 &nbsp;

00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:53.340
In 2022, it signed several agreements&nbsp;
with Kiribati, [Pron: Ki Ree BAS] which,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.340 --> 00:06:57.420
believe it or not, has one of the largest&nbsp;
exclusive economic zones in the world. Meanwhile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.420 --> 00:07:01.620
in Fiji they are carrying out projects&nbsp;
such as the Wanguo Friendship Plaza,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:03.120
the tallest tower in the South Pacific.
 &nbsp;

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:06.600
These are just two examples. We even find&nbsp;
that China, in addition to mobilizing a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:10.260
hospital ship, is also handing out a lot of&nbsp;
scholarships for students in the region.
 &nbsp;

00:07:10.260 --> 00:07:15.240
So the question is, what is China after? What&nbsp;
does it hope to get in return for all this aid?
 &nbsp;

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:23.580
Clearly influence. But, keep in mind, we are&nbsp;
not only talking about military ties such as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.580 --> 00:07:26.880
those being developed with the Solomon Islands.&nbsp;
We are also talking about a political agenda:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:30.900
for example, the firm and unwavering support&nbsp;
for the Asian giant in spaces such as the United&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.900 --> 00:07:35.700
Nations. You could say that for China it is a&nbsp;
relatively cheap way to win a lot of votes.
 &nbsp;

00:07:35.700 --> 00:07:39.300
And, mind you, we are not only talking about&nbsp;
cooperation or agreements at government level.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.300 --> 00:07:44.040
Many Chinese companies such as ZTE or Huawei are&nbsp;
also trying to get a foothold in this region.
 &nbsp;

00:07:44.040 --> 00:07:47.880
Of course, wait a minute, because&nbsp;
not everyone trusts Beijing.
 &nbsp;

00:07:47.880 --> 00:07:51.960
In some cases Chinese activities on these&nbsp;
islands are fueling ethnic and social tensions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:55.440
Corruption, intensive fishing by Chinese&nbsp;
fishing vessels and fears about loss of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.440 --> 00:07:59.640
sovereignty are not making everyone happy&nbsp;
with this association. Which is precisely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.640 --> 00:08:03.720
what explains news stories like this:
 
N4(25 November 2021: Solomon Islands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.720 --> 00:08:07.500
experiences second day of protests with&nbsp;
attacks on Chinese businesses – Euronews)
 &nbsp;

00:08:07.500 --> 00:08:11.400
And, of course, China is still far from&nbsp;
winning this race. Why? Because Uncle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:13.980
Sam has woken up.
 
Let’s take a look.
 &nbsp;

00:08:18.120 --> 00:08:25.680
T3(THE RED HOT COMPETITION)
 
Not since the Second World&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:29.640
War has the South Pacific been in such&nbsp;
high demand. Following China's landing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.640 --> 00:08:32.760
now Australia and the United States&nbsp;
have stepped up to the plate.
 &nbsp;

00:08:32.760 --> 00:08:35.820
Now, how do you think they are&nbsp;
doing it? Well, basically by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.820 --> 00:08:42.240
pulling out the checkbook. Take a look.
 
N5(US pledges $210mn to Pacific Islands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:46.140
to counter China's influence – FT)
 
And not only that. In February 2022,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.140 --> 00:08:50.220
US Secretary of State, Anthony Blinken,&nbsp;
visited Fiji. It was the first time in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.220 --> 00:08:54.840
36 years that a secretary of state had traveled&nbsp;
here. Uncle Sam has also announced the reopening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.840 --> 00:08:58.860
of embassies in Tonga and the Solomon Islands,&nbsp;
in addition to re-establishing the US Agency&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.860 --> 00:09:03.060
for International Development in Fiji.
 
But by far the biggest rapprochement took&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.060 --> 00:09:06.900
place in Washington, where a summit chaired by&nbsp;
President Joe Biden himself was held in the fall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.900 --> 00:09:12.420
of 2022 and where the US pledged to invest more&nbsp;
than $800 million in infrastructure projects.
 &nbsp;

00:09:12.420 --> 00:09:16.320
On the other hand, we should not forget that&nbsp;
although China has signed a security agreement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:20.220
with the Solomon Islands, the United States has&nbsp;
free association treaties with three countries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.220 --> 00:09:24.420
in this region: the Marshall Islands, the Republic&nbsp;
of Palau and the Federated States of Micronesia.
 &nbsp;

00:09:24.420 --> 00:09:28.140
Thanks to these agreements, the United States&nbsp;
can establish bases and control the access of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.140 --> 00:09:31.800
third countries to the territorial waters of&nbsp;
these three countries. These agreements have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.800 --> 00:09:36.120
to be renewed between this year and next, but&nbsp;
no doubt this will be fairly straightforward.
 &nbsp;

00:09:36.120 --> 00:09:41.460
And then, of course, relatively close behind are&nbsp;
the two heavyweights: Guam and American Samoa, two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.460 --> 00:09:46.320
territories that are already under US sovereignty&nbsp;
with a situation similar to that of Puerto Rico.
 &nbsp;

00:09:48.420 --> 00:09:53.640
In Guam, in fact, we can find a very significant&nbsp;
military presence. About a quarter of the island&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.640 --> 00:09:57.600
is under US military control. We are talking&nbsp;
about a naval base and an air base where more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.200 --> 00:10:01.740
6,000 soldiers are deployed and where we can find&nbsp;
advanced equipment such as nuclear submarines or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.740 --> 00:10:06.780
the F22, the crown jewel of the US air force.
 
For its part, Australia, in addition to once again&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.780 --> 00:10:10.920
taking relations with all these small countries&nbsp;
seriously, has an important ace up its sleeve:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.920 --> 00:10:15.360
It is still by far the one that writes the&nbsp;
biggest check. Take this chart, for example.
 &nbsp;

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:17.820
As you can see, China has&nbsp;
promised a lot of things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.820 --> 00:10:21.540
but when it comes to putting up the&nbsp;
dough, Australia is still way ahead.

00:10:21.540 --> 00:10:25.320
In other words, one way or another, no matter how&nbsp;
much progress China has made, the United States&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.320 --> 00:10:29.580
and Australia still have the upper hand.
 
Be that as it may, this is another of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.580 --> 00:10:32.760
many races that China and the United&nbsp;
States are waging around the world.
 &nbsp;

00:10:33.660 --> 00:10:37.740
But having come this far, it's your turn:&nbsp;
Who do you think will gain the upper hand in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.740 --> 00:10:40.740
the South Pacific? Have you ever thought&nbsp;
that this remote region could be on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.740 --> 00:10:44.640
international agenda? Have you ever imagined&nbsp;
yourself sailing around these idyllic islands?

00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:48.180
Leave us your thoughts in the comments. And&nbsp;
you know, if you found this video interesting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.180 --> 00:10:51.420
don't forget to like it and&nbsp;
subscribe to VisualPolitik&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.420 --> 00:10:54.780
if you haven't already done so.
 
All the best and see you next time.

